  "body": {
    "id": "prop:binary-alphabet-parametric-rate",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For \\(d=2\\) there are constants \\(0<c_\\epsilon\\le C_\\epsilon<\\infty\\) such that, uniformly over \\(n\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{c_\\epsilon}{n}\\le R_\\epsilon(n,m,2)\\le\\frac{C_\\epsilon}{n}.\n\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(R_\\epsilon(n,m,d)\\)",
      "\\(c_\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(C_\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(m\\)",
      "\\(N\\)",
      "\\(d\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:inverse-count-baseline",
      "thm:known-marginal-boundary",
      "def:minimax-risk",
      "def:known-marginal-risk"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Supplying the exact table \\(P_{XA}\\) is more informative than supplying \\(m\\) draws from it, so every estimator in the latter experiment can be used in the former by simulating and then discarding those draws.  Hence \\(R_\\epsilon(n,m,2)\\ge R_\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{known}}(n,2)\\ge c_\\epsilon/n\\) by \\(\\mathrm{thm:known\\mbox{-}marginal\\mbox{-}boundary}\\).  The baseline theorem and \\(N=n+m\\ge n\\) give\n\\[\n R_\\epsilon(n,m,2)\\le C_\\epsilon\\min\\{1,n^{-1}+4N^{-2}\\}\\le5C_\\epsilon/n.\n\\]",
